message:
Now I can see the vulnerability. Let me analyze `lex_multiline_string`:

```c
static void *lex_multiline_string(struct lexer *lexer)
{
    char *end = (peek(lexer) == '"') ? "\"\"\"" : "'''";

    advance_n(lexer, strlen("'''") - 1);

    do {
        if (!strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)) {
            emit(lexer, LEXEME_STRING);
            lexer->pos += 3;

            return lex_config;
        }
    } while (next(lexer) != '\0');

    return lex_error(lexer, "EOF while scanning multiline string");
}
```

The issue: `strncmp(lexer->pos, end, 3)` reads 3 bytes from `lexer->pos`. If the input ends with `'''` or `"""` (or even just 1-2 bytes remaining before the end of the buffer), `strncmp` will read past the end of the buffer, causing a global-buffer-overflow READ.

Let me look at the `next`, `peek`, `advance_n`, and `backup` functions to understand the buffer handling:

<｜｜DSML｜｜=execute_bash>
<parameter=command>sed -n '1,300p' /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/lwan/src/lib/lwan-config.c</｜｜DSML｜｜>

